#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Entry point. Everything an operator runs starts here, at the project root.

Role: entry point
Reads: nothing directly
Writes: nothing directly
Can move tokens: YES -- it dispatches to commands that do
Live-safe: depends on the subcommand; `status` and `verify` are read-only

DELIBERATELY THIN. The layering this repo follows is entry point -> module ->
function, with the decisions at the bottom where they can be called with seeded
inputs and asserted on directly. Everything here is dispatch; the commands live
in `gigajoule_core/cli.py` and the decisions they reach live further down still.

IT IS AT THE ROOT BECAUSE THAT IS WHERE A RUNNABLE THING IS FINDABLE. A script
buried three directories down gets invoked by a path that some launcher, cron
entry or shell alias hardcodes, and moving it then breaks a caller no import
graph can see. Root placement is discoverable by looking, which is the only
method that works for the callers that reference a file by name.

THERE IS NO `sys.path` INSERT HERE, AND THERE MUST NOT BE ONE. The first draft
had the usual three lines -- insert the script's directory, then import, then a
`noqa: E402` to quiet the lint that the reordering causes. All three were
unnecessary: when Python runs a script it already puts that script's own
directory at `sys.path[0]`, so the insert re-added a path that was there before
the interpreter reached the first line. Checked rather than assumed, by running
`./gigajoule` without it. The suppression existed to silence a rule that was
complaining, correctly, about code that did nothing.
"""

import sys

from gigajoule_core.cli import main

if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
